lv2 mutex/cond semantics: a cond signal hands the bound mutex to the woken waiter (owner = waiter if free, else queued on the
mutex and handed over at the unlock), as lv2 does; the waiter no longer re-locks it itself. The mutex is an lv2-style object (owner,
count, FIFO hand-off queue) on a short host lock + condition variable instead of a host CRITICAL_SECTION/pthread mutex. Destroy
follows lv2 (ESRCH, EBUSY while owned, EPERM while a cond is bound; cond create/destroy count the binding); create validates the
attributes (EFAULT, EINVAL); trylock by the owner of a non-recursive mutex is EDEADLK; signal_to wakes only the named thread
(EPERM if it does not wait); slots are allocated next-fit and their host locks are never destroyed. Root cause of ToD's intermittent
NEW GAME level-load stall: the main thread's trylock of the TIER loader mutex succeeded in the window between its own signal and
the loader's re-lock, then blocked in sys_mutex_lock while the loader waited for a completion only the main thread sends
(rc-runtime pass 11, the RE notes section 7). ASSUMPTION: FIFO hand-off for every protocol (lv2 orders PRIORITY waiters).
Host test: port/rc/tests/run_lv2_sync_test.sh. Upstream candidate.
